=======================================================
2. EK SINGLE DATABASE BOTTLENECK KYUN BAN JAATA HAI?
=======================================================

Small applications ke liye ek single database sufficient hota hai.

Application ke saare database operations (READ and WRITE)
isi database par jaate hain:

    User Request
        |
        v
    Application Server
        |
        v
    Single Database
        |
        v
    Response

Initially ye architecture simple aur sufficient hota hai.

Problem tab start hoti hai jab application ka traffic aur data grow karta hai.

-------------------------------
## 1. DATABASE CPU BOTTLENECK
-------------------------------

Database queries execute karne ke liye CPU use hota hai.

Agar bahut saari queries simultaneously execute ho rahi hain,
to database server ka CPU overloaded ho sakta hai.

Result:

    More queries
        ↓
    Higher CPU utilization
        ↓
    Higher query latency
        ↓
    Application becomes slower


--------------------------------
## 2. RAM / DISK BOTTLENECK
--------------------------------

Database jis server par deployed/run ho raha hota hai,
us server par database ko certain amount of RAM aur
persistent storage (Disk/SSD) available hota hai.

RAM database caching, query execution aur other database
operations ke liye use hoti hai.

Similarly, Disk/SSD database ke persistent data ko store
karne ke liye use hota hai.

Agar RAM ya Disk resources insufficient ho jaayein,
to database bottleneck ban sakta hai:

    More queries
        ↓
    Memory constraint
          ↓
    Higher Query Latency
          ↓
    Application becomes slower


----------------------------------------------------
## 3. DATABASE CONNECTIONS CAN BECOME A BOTTLENECK
----------------------------------------------------

Suppose application configuration mein:

    Maximum DB connections = 100

Aur 100 connections already busy hain.

Agar 101st request ko database access karna ho, 
to usse connection pool mein available connection ke liye wait karna padega.
Agar configured timeout ke andar connection available nahi hua, to connection timeout ho sakta hai.


----------------------
## 4. TOO MANY READS
----------------------

Jab application par bahut zyada read requests aane lagti hain,
to single database par read load increase ho jata hai.

Agar application par read traffic database ki available capacity se zyada ho jaata hai, 
to database par load increase hone lagta hai, jiski wajah se query latency badh sakti hai 
aur application slow ho sakti hai.

Frequently requested data ko Redis jaise cache mein store karke
repeated database reads ko reduce kiya ja sakta hai,
especially jab same data repeatedly read ho raha ho.

Lekin caching har type ke read workload ko completely solve nahi karti.

For example:

    - Data frequently change hota hai,
      to cache invalidation ka overhead aa sakta hai.

    - Requests mostly unique data ke liye hain,
      to cache misses zyada ho sakte hain.

Aise cases mein database ko still significant read traffic
handle karna pad sakta hai.

Isliye large read-heavy systems mein caching ke saath
database ko bhi scale kiya ja sakta hai.

Database side par multiple Read Replicas use karke
read traffic ko distribute kiya ja sakta hai.

    Caching
        +
    Database Read Scaling

large read-heavy workloads ko handle karne ke liye
better approach ho sakti hai.


---------------------
5. TOO MANY WRITES
---------------------

Database write operations bhi expensive ho sakte hain, kyunki writes ke time data persist karne ke saath 
logging, locking aur transaction handling jaise additional operations bhi perform ho sakte hain.

Agar same database par bahut zyada reads aur writes aa rahe hain, to database ke resources heavily consume ho sakte hain, 
jisse query latency increase ho sakti hai aur application ka response slow ho sakta hai.

Writes ko simply multiple database nodes par distribute karna straightforward nahi hota, 
kyunki concurrent writes ke case mein data consistency aur conflict handling difficult ho sakti hai.

Read-heavy workloads mein hum Read Replicas use kar sakte hain:

Writes → Primary DB
Reads  → Read Replicas

Isse read traffic Primary DB se offload ho jaata hai, jisse Primary DB ki capacity mainly write workload ke liye available rehti hai.

## IMPORTANT:
--------------

Read Replicas ka purpose writes ko directly faster banana nahi hai.
Ye primarily read workload ko Primary DB se offload karne ke liye use hote hain.


------------------------------
## 6. INCREASING DATA SIZE
------------------------------

Jaise-jaise application par users increase hote hain, 
single database par stored data bhi continuously grow karta hai.

Data size bahut large hone par kuch read queries ko larger dataset se required data 
process karna pad sakta hai, jiski wajah se query latency increase ho sakti hai.

Read queries ko fast karne ke liye indexes use kiye ja sakte hain, 
lekin indexes ko bhi database mein store karne ke liye additional disk space required hota hai.

Saath hi, continuously increasing data ke liye backups maintain karne mein bhi
additional disk space aur CPU processing required hoti hai.

Isliye data size increase hone ke saath database par additional 
resource pressure create ho sakta hai.



